hi i'm alex pasek and i clean drains  today we're going to look at a few  drains and i'm going to show you how to  clean them when a drain is clogged the  obvious problem is that it backs up and  water doesn't drain which can cause  flooding which can lead to the drain  backing up further down  its run where it's even harder to to get  a clog out of so regular maintenance of  grains is really important to avoid  those types of things  here we've got a basin drain the water  runs through the grate on the top falls  down into this basin and the water that  falls in brings with it any of the dirt  or sand or whatever else it picks up  with it into that basin so the first  step i do is i pop off the grate top i  see that it's very backed up with the  soil and sand so i start digging just  trying to expose as much of the drain as  i can i see that there's the exit pipe  and around it is a lot of roots uh mixed  in with the sand so i start sort of kind  of break away at those eventually  getting down and pulling out the roots  from the pipe i get further down into  the basin and finally hit the bottom  where the roots are all coiled and i'm  able to pull out those roots all in a  few big pieces and then i  give the drain a quick  scrape out and clean and then pop the  lid back on and that's good to go  so french trains are typically at the  bottom of a sloped driveway and the  reason for that is if a driveway is on a  slope and the house is at the end of the  slope water will continue on the slope  and end up in the house so the french  drains are put there as a way to divert  the water away from the house first i'm  just gonna take off the top i already  can see that there's quite a lot of  debris in here so the first thing i'm to  do is just get my shovel and start  digging it out there's all types of  different creatures living in here some  worms some bags the debris is made up  mostly of soil and sand and pine needles  so  everything that's good for blocking the  drain is in there  then i'm going to start cleaning the  pipe that that exits this drain and  obviously this is important because no  matter how clean the drain is we're  relying on that pipe to bring out the  water  and i don't stop doing that until either  i can sort of see light through the end  of that pipe or until like i'm pretty  certain by shoving something in there  that it is now cleaned out and then i  get out the pressure washer  give the whole chamber and the top of it  a clean which just allows for better  flow and a nicer appearance as well  after that i pop the grates back on and  we're looking at a clean french drain  it's important to keep an eye on the  level of debris in french drains um they  can fill up quite quickly even in the  dryer months it's easy just to look in  and see okay there's a little bit of  stuff in here i can no longer see the  complete face of the  drain it's probably time to open it up  and just see how deep this is and give  it a quick scoop out  these chambers are fairly simple to  clean and it's quite easy just to get a  shovel and start scooping that debris  out this drain didn't have any roots  growing into it which is a good sign  that means that  this debris hasn't probably been there  too too long  a couple years maybe once all of that  muck is scooped out of there i  took the opportunity while it was all  open and and free of debris to actually  give it a good clean so  i got a pressure washer there and was  able just to  clean off the sides of the drain and  that just allows for better flow through  the whole drain as the water  often comes in at the side not actually  right through the top so the smoother of  a surface for the water to go in just  allows a better  all-over flow for the drain  i would say um  you know don't be afraid to to open your  drains and take a look in there it's not  so scary it's probably nothing you can  break in there um really they are quite  simple generally just chambers that fill  up with soil no harm in opening them up  and starting to dig
 

